# Model Comparison

Five simple linear regression models and one multiple linear regression model were developed to identify the factors associated with monthly sales. The models were compared using R-squared values, statistical significance (p-values), and their usefulness for business decision-making.

| Model Name                           | Dependent Variable | Independent Variable(s)                                            | R²     | Significant Variables                                  | Business Usefulness                                                                                                         | Limitations                                                                                                                         |
| ------------------------------------ | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------ | ------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Model 1: Marketing Spend             | Monthly Sales      | Marketing Spend                                                    | 0.1672 | Marketing Spend (p < 0.05)                             | Useful for understanding the impact of marketing investment on sales.                                                       | Explains only 16.72% of the variation in sales and ignores other important factors.                                                 |
| Model 2: Footfall                    | Monthly Sales      | Footfall                                                           | 0.7363 | Footfall (p < 0.05)                                    | Highly useful because customer traffic is a strong predictor of monthly sales.                                              | Does not consider marketing, discounts, inventory, or regional differences.                                                         |
| Model 3: Average Discount Percentage | Monthly Sales      | Average Discount Percentage                                        | 0.0083 | Not Significant (p > 0.05)                             | Limited business value because discounts alone do not explain sales effectively.                                            | Very low explanatory power and statistically insignificant relationship.                                                            |
| Model 4: Inventory Availability      | Monthly Sales      | Inventory Availability Percentage                                  | 0.0131 | Inventory Availability (p < 0.05)                      | Provides limited insight into inventory's contribution to sales.                                                            | Explains only a very small proportion of sales variation.                                                                           |
| Model 5: Customer Rating             | Monthly Sales      | Customer Rating                                                    | 0.0042 | Not Significant (p > 0.05)                             | Not useful as a standalone predictor of monthly sales.                                                                      | Very weak explanatory power and statistically insignificant.                                                                        |
| Model 6: Multiple Regression         | Monthly Sales      | Marketing Spend, Footfall, Average Discount Percentage, West Dummy | 0.7873 | Marketing Spend, Footfall, Average Discount Percentage | Most useful model because it considers multiple business factors simultaneously and provides the highest explanatory power. | Does not include every possible factor affecting sales, such as competition, local economic conditions, seasonality, or promotions. |

## Comparison Summary
Among the simple regression models, Footfall performed the best with an R² value of 0.7363, explaining approximately 73.63% of the variation in monthly sales. Marketing Spend also showed a statistically significant positive relationship but explained only 16.72% of the variation.
Average Discount Percentage and Customer Rating were weak individual predictors due to their very low explanatory power. Inventory Availability was statistically significant but explained only a small portion of sales variation.
The Multiple Regression model performed the best overall with an R² of 0.7873, explaining 78.73% of the variation in monthly sales. Marketing Spend, Footfall, and Average Discount Percentage remained statistically significant predictors, while the West Dummy variable was not statistically significant. Therefore, the multiple regression model is selected as the final model because it provides the strongest predictive performance and better supports business decision-making.

